+\subsection{Verification, Validation, and Domain of Validity}
+\label{sec:vv-structure}
+
+The study separates five forms of evidence. First, implementation
+verification consists of simulator and policy unit tests, schema/structural
+validators for campaign outputs, the repeated-run determinism check, and
+policy-specific fidelity audits where a policy is labeled
+\texttt{FAITHFUL\_EXTERNAL} (\S\ref{sec:fidelity}). Second, input/workload
+validity is supported by using independently released traces, preserving
+source-native provenance where fields exist, and characterizing the frozen
+workload corpus rather than assuming source interchangeability
+(\S\ref{sec:workloads}). Third, experimental-design validity is provided by
+the preregistered freeze of policies, windows, operating regions, metrics,
+and analysis rules before comparative outcomes were generated, together
+with the paired design and outcome-independent policy-selection rule
+(\S\ref{sec:study-design}). Fourth, physical-system validation is limited
+to the selected vLLM/GPU cases in \S\ref{sec:real-system}: the goal is to
+test relative ordering and reversal direction for those cases, not to
+validate absolute simulated latencies or every cell in the matrix. Fifth,
+the domain of validity is therefore the tested simulator, workload windows,
+policy panel, operating regions, metrics, and selected validation cases; no
+claim is made that the simulator is globally accurate for all LLM-serving
+systems or that every simulated reversal would transfer to physical
+hardware.
 
